---
// About — built to the dealt look (docs/pare/full-redesign/deal.md) with the
// words of copy.md (About page), as a stack of screens (the kit's layout-v4.md):
//   1. hero        split: the h1 and lead on span-6 (the site's 6/6 pair), one
//                  portrait on span-6 start-7 with one flat float card (1 screen).
//                  The hero was dealt film, then photo; both rejected because every
//                  kept portrait is 724px wide (film's frame is 1280, photo wants
//                  1920), so the deal fell to split, the one hero the material fits.
//   2. #story      the 3gforfree story left, the real site screenshot and its
//                  facts in a panel on the surface step            (1 screen)
//   3. #market     the dealt moment word-highlight (seed ee67adf3): the story's
//                  closing line as the page's one statement          (1 screen)
//   4. #career     split: Disney to Pinterest left, a portrait right  (1 screen)
//   5. #roles      the parish, board, livestream and AYSO rows        (1 screen)
//   6. footer.closing (SiteFooter): close-photo + footer-dark         (1 screen)
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { BOOK_CALL } from '../data/site.js';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Ian Dunn: from 3gforfree.com to Pinterest"
  description="How eight years running a ringtone business led Ian Dunn into engineering at Disney, NBC, Netflix, Spotify and Pinterest."
  moment="word-highlight"
>
  <!-- type: service
       deal: hero split (film and photo dealt first, both rejected: no kept portrait is wide
             enough for their frames), nav nav-centre, close close-photo, footer footer-dark,
             rhythm surface-pairs (the band stays on Home; here every section sits on the
             page ground and the panel takes the surface step), buttons arrow, mark initial
       moment: word-highlight in #market (the statement that turns the story) -->
  <SiteNav active="About" />

  <main id="top">
    <section class="hero hero--split" data-enter aria-labelledby="hero-h">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="hero-text grid hero__grid">
        <div class="hero__text span-6">
          <h1 id="hero-h">Engineering manager who started as a founder</h1>
          <p class="hero__lead">I run an engineering team at Pinterest and live in Los Angeles with my wife, Dr.&nbsp;Raven Barrow, and our three kids.</p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href={BOOK_CALL.url}>{BOOK_CALL.label}<svg class="m-arrow" viewBox="0 0 12 12" aria-hidden="true"><path class="m-arrow__stem" d="M1 6h8"></path><path class="m-arrow__head" d="M4.5 2.5 8 6l-3.5 3.5"></path></svg></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__media span-6 start-7">
          <img src="/photos/pinterest-seated-full.jpg" alt="Ian Dunn seated on a stool in front of a painted arch at Pinterest" width="724" height="1086" style="--ly-pos: 50% 42%" fetchpriority="high" />
          <div class="ly-float">
            <div class="ly-float__title">Livestream producer, Sundays at 10 a.m.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="story" aria-labelledby="story-h" style="--h: 1; --hm: 2">
      <div class="wrap grid grid--top">
        <div class="span-6 stack-6">
          <h2 id="story-h">3gforfree.com, my first business</h2>
          <p>In the early days of the mobile web I worked at Sprint. Coworkers wanted ringtones and screensavers for their flip phones, so I worked out how to deliver them and put up a site on a computer at my parents&rsquo; house.</p>
          <p>They passed the link to customers, the traffic grew, and a favor became my full-time work for about eight years, through my twenties.</p>
          <p>I built both halves: the website, and a mobile site that fingerprinted each phone so it only got files it could play. There was no cloud to scale on, so every slowdown got fixed by hand. I also learned the business side: what customers asked for, music and content rights, and running a whole system on my own.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="story__panel span-6 start-7">
          <img src="/3gforfree-site.png" alt="The 3gforfree.com homepage as it ran" width="150" height="139" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <dl class="story__facts">
            <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Founder and operator</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Span</dt><dd>About eight years, through my twenties</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Drupal and CodeIgniter</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Device detection</dt><dd>WURFL, fingerprinting each phone</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- word-highlight (moments.md): the statement rests at half ink and turns to ink
         word by word as it crosses the middle of the screen -->
    <section class="section statement" id="market" data-moment="word-highlight" aria-label="How it ended">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="t-label">How it ended</p>
        <p class="mo-wh__text">Then the carriers and platforms took over ringtone sales. The market moved, and I moved into <strong>engineering jobs</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="career" aria-labelledby="career-h" style="--h: 1; --hm: 2">
      <div class="wrap grid grid--top">
        <div class="span-5 stack-6">
          <h2 id="career-h">Disney, NBC, Netflix, Spotify and Pinterest</h2>
          <p>After 3gforfree came engineering jobs, then management. For more than two years I&rsquo;ve run a small team inside Pinterest&rsquo;s ad sales organization, building internal tools for the account teams who manage some of the largest advertisers in the world.</p>
          <p>The team has no product manager, designer or program manager. The engineers talk to the people who use our tools and decide what to build. I wrote about how that works.</p>
          <p class="career__links">
            <a class="link" href="/writing/build-it-to-run-without-you/">Read Build it to run without you</a>
            <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/iancdunn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See my roles on LinkedIn</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="frame frame--photo career__photo span-6 start-7">
          <img src="/photos/pinterest-seated-close.jpg" alt="Ian Dunn seated in glasses and a grey hoodie at Pinterest" width="724" height="1086" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="roles" aria-labelledby="roles-h" style="--h: 1; --hm: 2">
      <div class="wrap grid grid--top">
        <div class="span-6 roles__text">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2 id="roles-h">Church, a board seat and Saturday soccer</h2>
          <p>Most of my week outside Pinterest and the apps goes to four roles in Los Angeles.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="rows roles__rows">
          <li>
            <span class="rows__name">People&rsquo;s Warden</span>
            <span class="rows__value">Christ the Good Shepherd, Episcopal</span>
            <p>Elected to represent the congregation on the vestry, the parish&rsquo;s governing board.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rows__name">Board member</span>
            <span class="rows__value">Good Shepherd Manor, Leimert Park</span>
            <p>Affordable housing for seniors in Leimert Park.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rows__name">Livestream producer</span>
            <span class="rows__value">Sundays at 10 a.m.</span>
            <p>I built the parish&rsquo;s broadcast setup and automated the thumbnails, bulletins and clips. It goes live every Sunday and runs without me.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rows__name">AYSO regional referee</span>
            <span class="rows__value">U-12, most Saturdays</span>
            <p>I referee in my kids&rsquo; league. Center Ref started on that field.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="frame frame--photo roles__photo span-6 start-7">
          <img src="/photos/pinterest-half.jpg" alt="Ian Dunn standing in a grey hoodie in front of a painted arch" width="724" height="1086" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* the story panel: the real 3gforfree screenshot (a ~150px artifact of the
     real site, held under the checker's 1.33x blow-up line) and the facts, on
     the contrast step the surface band leaves (--surface is the page ground here) */
  .story__panel {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    padding: var(--s-7);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-6);
    align-content: start;
    align-self: start;
  }
  .story__panel > img {
    width: 196px;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: #fff;
  }
  .story__facts {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-5);
  }
  .story__facts > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-1);
  }
  .story__facts dt {
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .story__facts dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  /* the statement's label sits a step above its line */
  #market .t-label {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
  }

  /* the career portrait: 1:1 on the split's right column (layout-v4.md, Pictures) */
  .career__photo {
    margin: 0;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .career__photo img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% 24%;
  }

  /* the two career links stack as rows */
  .career__links {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    justify-items: start;
  }

  /* the roles portrait mirrors the career one on the same split line */
  .roles__photo {
    margin: 0;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .roles__photo img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% 20%;
  }

  /* the tablet (layout-v4.md, the tablet): stacking either split would run past
     its screen, so both keep their two columns on the page's 6/6 pair */
  @media (min-width: 640px) and (max-width: 1023px) {
    :is(#story, #roles) .grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    :is(#story, #roles) .grid > .span-6 {
      grid-column: auto / span 6;
    }
    :is(#story, #roles) .grid > .start-7 {
      grid-column: 7 / span 6;
    }
    /* the narrower columns hold fewer words a line: a step up keeps the fill */
    #story .stack-6 > p {
      font-size: 18px;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .story__panel {
      padding: var(--s-5);
    }
    .roles__photo {
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    }
    .roles__rows > li {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .roles__rows .rows__value {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: auto;
      text-align: left;
      white-space: normal;
    }
    .career__photo {
      margin-top: var(--s-7);
      aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
    }
  }

  /* the phone: each section holds its two screens with its content spread
     through them (layout-v4.md: gaps between sections stay in the 112-420 band) */
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    /* flush at the phone width, so its text sits on the container's line */
    .story__panel {
      margin-top: var(--s-7);
      padding: 0;
      background: none;
      border-radius: 0;
      gap: var(--s-7);
    }
    .story__facts {
      gap: var(--s-7);
    }
    /* the portrait takes what the text leaves of the two screens, so the
       section ends on the picture, not an empty band (as the hero does) */
    #career .wrap.grid {
      height: 100%;
      grid-template-rows: minmax(0, auto) minmax(240px, 1fr);
      align-items: stretch;
    }
    .career__photo {
      margin-top: var(--s-7);
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      height: calc(100% - var(--s-7));
    }
    .roles__photo {
      margin-top: var(--s-7);
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    }
    .roles__rows > li {
      padding-block: var(--s-5);
    }
    /* wider than half the 342px wrap: run the wrap's full width (SR-6) */
    .career__links > a {
      width: 100%;
    }
    .story__panel > img {
      width: 160px;
    }
  }
</style>
